Findings. No finding is blocking under the PM round cap.

F1 is the serious one. The new exe-path filter in daemon_pids skips the live
daemon shape. The live incident shows argv[0] as the absolute opencode.exe
under node_modules, outside every job root. I ran the extracted production
block over a synthetic proc table with that argv[0] and HOME and cwd inside the
job root. It selected no pid. The P4B-C1 local control uses a bare `node`
argv[0], so it cannot fail on the real shape. The exit-time
opencode_host_cleanup may still stop the daemon by the BENCH_OPENCODE_JOB
marker. The per-pass guard and its INFRA record no longer match. Fix: key the
scope on the BENCH_OPENCODE_JOB marker, and add a case with a path-form exe
outside the job root.

F2: the judge shell decoder keeps a top-level result event text in `parts`.
The two TS decoders drop it when text events exist. My node comparison shows
the divergence. The three comments still say `the same decoder`. The N13 judge
fixture feeds the shell a different shape.

F3: the N32 case sits only in quota.test.ts. The strategy names the two
runner files too. review-run.test.ts:1895 only checks the word `quota`.

F4: the N26 case asserts only `<=500` and does not run a probe or write a
record. An empty line would pass.

F5: the judge-run copies of N10 and N27 are not pinned. The N10 walk refuses
any symlink under a call path, not only one that leaves the root.

F6: plan.md:2503-2505 still says a failed scan prints the found-key text. Task
4.15 N31 changes that path.

Hygiene: I started no server and ran no opencode and no openchamber. pgrep for
fake-opencode and quota-server printed nothing of mine. I changed no
production file, no test, and no plan. I wrote only the two result files.

Handoff: F1 is a regression of the opencode_host_isolation fault. It needs a
fix before the 2026-11-22 night run. I recommend a small Red/Green cycle, or a
remediation track if the PM keeps 4.15 open past the night run. Option A: fix
